tified poison causes incoordination, arched back, rapid pulse, gasping, trembling (known as 'the jimmies'), and prostration; in severe cases death can result from respiratory paralysis. Sheep are apparently most susceptible, followed by goats and cattle. A dosage of 0.8–3% of body weight for 2–3 days has been shown to be toxic. (Weathers 1998; Burrows &amp; Tyrl 2001)." />
