        <Item Id="28" Name="Botrychium virginianum (L.) Sw." Img="#24" Href="http://ferns.brit.org/?q=node/82">
            <Description>Plant erect, 8–75 cm tall; roots 15 or fewer, yellow to brown; leaves seasonal, appearing in early spring and dying in summer; sterile blade portion pale green, sessile, thin, herbaceous, 4–30 cm long and wide, 3–5-pinnate-pinnatifid; ultimate leaf segments linear, with midvein present, serrate to lacerate, pointed at apex; 2n = 184 (Wagner &amp; Wagner 1993). Moist, rich woods and thickets; Pineywoods w to Cross Timbers and Prairies (e.g., Montague and Tarrant cos., BRIT; Coryell Co., TEX-LL); also e Edwards Plateau (e.g., Gillespie Co., O’Kennon 11288, Bear Mt., BRIT; Kerr Co., Palmer 1919b); throughout most of Canada and the U.S.; also Mexico to South America, Europe, and Asia (Mickel &amp; Smith 2004). Spores maturing Apr–Jun. [Botrypus virginianus (L.) Holub] If Botrychium is divided into segregate genera, as argued by Hauk et al. (2003), B. virginianum would be recognized in the genus Botrypus. The common name is probably derived from a resemblance of the clusters of sporangia to the rattles of a rattlesnake. Alternatively, it has 'been reported that a salve made by boiling the roots of the plant was used by the Cherokee Indians in the treatment of snake bites' (Nelson 2000).</Description>
            <Facets>
                <Facet Name="Additional important characteristics">
                    <String Value="Sterile blade sessile, thin, and herbaceous" />
                </Facet>
