nesseensis Shaver (Haufler et al. 1990). In the sw U.S. C. bulbifera has hybridized with C. reevesiana giving rise to the tetraploid C. utahensis. The various hybrids usually have less glandular pubescence than C. bulbifera, are typically widest beyond the base, and have misshapen bulblets (Haufler et al. 1993). Because of morphological similarities and because both are tetraploids with one parent in common, C. tennesseensis (in the e U.S.) has sometimes been incorrectly treated as a synonym of the genetically distinct C. utahensis (of the w U.S.) (e.g., The Plant List 2011; Tropicos 2011). Because of its rareness and limited distribution in the state, we consider this species to be of conservation concern in TX." />
